# 1. Title

**Generalizing Neural-LAM to Probabilistic Forecasting Models**

---

# 2. Abstract

Weather is inherently chaotic — a single deterministic forecast, no matter how accurate, cannot fully capture the range of possible atmospheric outcomes. The scientific community is increasingly recognizing that **probabilistic ensemble forecasting** is the path forward for actionable weather prediction. Research prototypes like **Graph-EFM** (Oskarsson et al., 2024) and **Diffusion-LAM** (2025) have demonstrated the immense potential of generative machine learning for producing spatially coherent ensemble forecasts, yet these innovations remain isolated in research branches and cannot be easily integrated into the main neural-lam codebase.

This proposal addresses that gap. I will **restructure the neural-lam model architecture** to natively support both deterministic and probabilistic forecasting paradigms. The work involves:

1. **Refactoring the model class hierarchy** — decomposing the monolithic [ARModel](file:///c:/Users/aftab/Desktop/neural-lam/neural_lam/models/ar_model.py#24-771) into composable components (`ForecasterModule`, `Forecaster`, `ARForecaster`, `StepPredictor`) following the design proposed in Issue [#49](https://github.com/mllam/neural-lam/issues/49), enabling non-autoregressive and ensemble-based model formulations.

2. **Implementing ensemble-aware metrics** — adding sample-based CRPS, ensemble spread, rank histograms, and reliability diagrams to the metrics module, moving beyond the current deterministic-only evaluation.

3. **Building ensemble visualization tools** — creating spatial ensemble mean/spread plots, spaghetti plots, and animated ensemble evolution displays that integrate with the existing plotting infrastructure.

4. **Integrating a reference probabilistic model** — porting the Graph-EFM model from the `prob_model_lam` branch (Issue [#62](https://github.com/mllam/neural-lam/issues/62)) into the new architecture as a concrete demonstration of the generalized framework.

The result will be a neural-lam that is **architecturally ready** for the probabilistic future of neural weather prediction — extensible, well-tested, and documented.

---

# 3. Benefits to the Community

### For the MLLAM Community
- **Unblocks research integration**: Probabilistic models like Graph-EFM and Diffusion-LAM can finally be merged into the main repository, eliminating the current "big discrepancy" with how the codebase is structured.
- **Reduces duplication**: Researchers currently maintain separate forks for probabilistic work. A generalized framework means future probabilistic models slot into the same codebase.
- **Accelerates innovation**: By providing ensemble-aware training, evaluation, and visualization out-of-the-box, researchers can focus on novel model architectures rather than boilerplate infrastructure.

### For National Weather Services & Operational Forecasters
- **Better uncertainty quantification**: Ensemble forecasts from neural models can complement or enhance traditional NWP ensembles, providing uncertainty information at a fraction of the computational cost.
- **Regional high-resolution ensembles**: Neural-LAM's focus on Limited Area Modeling means this enables high-resolution probabilistic forecasts for specific regions — exactly what national weather services need.

### For the Open-Source ML-Weather Community
- **Reference implementation**: A clean, well-documented probabilistic forecasting framework benefits the broader community working on ML weather prediction (GraphCast, Pangu-Weather, GenCast, etc).
- **Reproducible research**: Standardized metrics (CRPS, spread-skill) and visualization tools make it easier to compare and reproduce probabilistic forecasting experiments.

### For Students & New Contributors
- **Lower barrier to entry**: The refactored class hierarchy with clear separation of concerns makes the codebase far more approachable than the current monolithic [ARModel](file:///c:/Users/aftab/Desktop/neural-lam/neural_lam/models/ar_model.py#24-771).
- **Educational value**: A codebase that explicitly shows deterministic vs. probabilistic forecasting patterns serves as a learning resource for ML weather prediction.

